00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:12.310 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  my name is sarah drew and i am an art  conservator at center art studio in new  york city my team and i are going to  walk you through step by step how we  restore  a  250 plus year old french plaster and  gilt wood frame this frame was brought  to us by a private client along with a  painting that we will restore as well  the frame when it came to us was  extremely dirty  and it needed a lot of structural  repairs there was a lot of ornament that  was missing  [Music]  when we start cleaning the frame  we start with the back there's a lot of  resin on the back of the frame so we use  a scalpel  to scrape this resin off it's likely  some sort of polyurethane from  a previous restoration that stuff is  tricky to remove so  mechanically removing it with a scalpel  is the best approach  [Music]  once we have the back clean we flip the  frame right side up and we start the  front cleaning  [Music]  we commonly use cotton swabs to clean  the surface of a frame  but we also are going to be using micro  metal tools literally the same tools  that a dentist will use to really get  into all of the small little recesses  we use a variety of solvents which lift  the dirt up and allow us to then take  suaves to lift the dirt away  this frame was originally  water gilded which means  gold leaf was applied to it because of  that we have to be very careful with our  cleaning because  it would be very easy to remove that  original gilding  we never want to make something look  brand new  so  while we are going into the recesses and  we are removing the dirt  we don't want to entirely remove all of  that built up patina we want to retain  an element of age  [Music]  the main issue with the back of this  frame  is that it has been expanded to take a  larger piece of art than it was  originally built for  the first time we saw it we knew that we  were going to have to disassemble it and  build it back up properly  in order to  put the frame back properly we are using  a product called fish glue which is a  super condensed  fish product it's a very traditional  adhesive to use in conservation and it's  very strong  and then we align the frame  [Music]  once we feel that all the structural  work has been completed we move on to  filling in the missing areas of the  frame  three of the four corners are completely  missing their corner ornament  luckily we have one corner that is  completely intact that works to our  advantage in this case because we will  be able to take a silicone mold of this  corner and then cast plaster replicas of  it  to make a mold off the frame we use a  silicone compound once you have it mixed  together you can lay it over  a part of the frame that you want to  replicate  [Music]  it cures over  the course of about 20 or 30 minutes  once it's fully cured you can carefully  peel it off the frame  [Music]  and you will have the imprint of the  shape of the frame that you want to  replicate in that mold  we are using plaster to cast the molds  so we mix up a batch of plaster  [Music]  and we pour it into the silicone mold  [Music]  once the plaster has dried we can pop  that out of the mold  to prepare the frame to receive the  plaster casts we need to  remove some of the plastic material  that's already on it  we use a hammer and chisel to  carve away a recess that will allow the  past piece to  sit flush in the frame  we will shape and finesse these cast  pieces to make sure that they fit  properly in the areas where we're going  to be applying them  we use sandpaper in different grits  [Music]  we are using fish glue again to adhere  the cast pieces onto the frame  [Music]  when we feel like the frame is  physically complete it is time to move  on to the color work  the first layer of paint that we are  applying to the frame  is a primer  this is a venetian red primer and that  just creates a very nice red base layer  for us to work off of  we are gold leafing the ornaments in the  corners of this frame this is a process  called gilding  the frame was originally  gilded with gold leaf  and so whenever possible we like to use  the original materials in our  restoration just so the object has as  much integrity to the original as  possible  the first step to gilding  after you've applied the base layer is  to apply  a sizing medium  and this is brushed onto the plaster  and it is allowed to dry for about 30  minutes  it gets tacky  and once it's tacky it's in the right  state to accept the gold leaf  it is very very delicate so you can't  touch it with your hands it will just  crumble  so in order to handle it karen one of  our team members is using a horse hair  brush and lay it on to the ornament that  we want to guilt  we also will use squirrel hair brushes  to brush it on and make sure that it's  adhering to all of the recesses and all  of the areas of the frame  [Music]  once the gold leaf is applied we move on  to the in painting process  and we are going to paint over the gold  leaf to tone it down slightly and also  overall of the fills that we've made on  the frame and we use a combination  of conservation colors which are  paints that are specifically designed  for conservation work  it is literally a trial and error  process often we will make a color we'll  have to adjust it correct it a little  bit  this is where the frame starts to really  look like one whole object again  the next step is to apply a layer of  shellac  and this just serves as a top coat it  protects all of our work it also makes  everything just have a cohesive finish  and sheen  [Music]  we're really happy with how this  restoration came out we have done a lot  to restore the structural integrity  and the visual integrity of this frame  and we hope the client can enjoy the  frame for years to come  [Music]  you

